#DESCRIPTION
Evolved electron pressure: FIELD-ALIGNED conduction across the gradient. Same
large Spitzer coefficient as PARAM.in.conduction, but now a weak magnetic field
points along y, perpendicular to the seeded x variation of the density and of
Te.

The conductivity is the field-aligned dyad kappa b b, so with b along y the
heat flux can only transport along y and cannot smooth the x dependence of Te.
The closure must therefore stay adiabatic (Pe proportional to rho^gamma), like
PARAM.in.adiabatic and unlike PARAM.in.conduction.

The guide field is deliberately tiny (1e-10 T, i.e. ~1e-2 of the field used in
tests/ohm) so that it fixes the conduction direction without magnetising the
ion dynamics: u_i x B stays negligible.

Diagnostic: over the plotted points, the relative spread of Pe/rho^gamma must
be much smaller than that of Pe/rho - i.e. conduction is suppressed.

#INITFROMSWMF
F                       initFromSWMF

#GEOMETRY
-8.0                    xMin  [code units; Lx = 16 d_i]
 8.0                    xMax
-0.05                   yMin
 0.05                   yMax
-0.05                   zMin
 0.05                   zMax

#NCELL
48                      nCellX
1                       nCellY
1                       nCellZ

#MAXBLOCKSIZE
48                      maxBlockSizeX
1                       maxBlockSizeY
1                       maxBlockSizeZ

#PERIODICITY
T                       isPeriodicX
T                       isPeriodicY
T                       isPeriodicZ

#SOLVEEM
F                       solveEM

#HYBRIDPIC
T                       useHybridPIC

#RESISTIVITY
0.0                     etaResistivity  [m^2/s, SI magnetic diffusivity]

Electron pressure gradient (ambipolar restoring force for the IAW).
electronDensity0 is in amu/cc, the SAME unit as the UNIFORMSTATE rho input, so
set it equal to rho here (both 1.0 amu/cc); the code auto-normalizes both to
code units so the adiabatic closure
p_e = electronDensity0*Te*(rho/electronDensity0)^gamma stays consistent.
#ELECTRONTEMPERATURE
62.8                    electronTemperature  [eV]
1.6666666666666667      electronGamma
1.0                     electronDensity0  [amu/cc, same as UNIFORMSTATE rho]

Evolve Pe with its own PDE. Same reduced kappa as PARAM.in.conduction, but the
field-aligned dyad suppresses the heat flux because B is perpendicular to
grad(Te).
#ELECTRONPRESSURE
T                       useElectronPressureEq
1.0e-19                 heatCondKappa0SI  [W/(m K^(7/2))]  reduced for this test
20.0                    coulombLog
T                       fieldAlignedConduction
1.0                     fieldAlignedFraction
1.0e-15                 fieldAlignedBMinSI  [T]  weaker = isotropic
0.0                     heatFluxLimiter
0.0                     peMin
T                       ambipolarInStages

B = 0 so the Hall term (J x B)/rho and the convection term -u_i x B both vanish;
The only remaining E source is the electron pressure gradient -grad(p_e)/rho.
#HALLTERM
F                       useHallTerm
#BSUBCYCLE
1                       nBSubcycle

Smoothing of the ion fluid moments (density and momentum) before the Ohm's law
(nSmoothMoments = 2 binomial passes).
#SMOOTHMOMENTS
T                       doSmoothMoments
2                       nSmoothMoments
0.5                     coefSmoothMoments

Smoothing of the nodal electric field after each hybrid field advance.
The ambipolar term E = -grad(p_e)/rho uses a central difference that does not
couple odd/even cells, so without this a grid-scale (Nyquist) checkerboard can
grow in E and feed back into the ion push.
#SMOOTHE
T                       doSmoothE
2                       nSmoothE

Single kinetic ion species (q = m = 1); the electron is an implicit
neutralizing massless fluid, so no electron particle species is present.
#PLASMA
1                       nSpecies
1.0                     mass
1.0                     charge

#UNIFORMSTATE
1.0                     rho [amu/cc]   Species 0: ions
0.0                     ux [km/s]
0.0                     uy [km/s]
0.0                     uz [km/s]
400000.0                T [K]
0.0                     bx [T]         weak guide field along Y, see below
1.0e-10                 by [T]         sets the conduction direction only
0.0                     bz [T]
0.0                     ex [V/m]
0.0                     ey [V/m]
0.0                     ez [V/m]

#TESTCASE
IonAcousticWave
0.20                    pert       relative density-perturbation amplitude (< 1)
1                       waveMode   number of wavelengths across the x-domain

#TIMESTEPPING
T                       useFixedDt
0.02                    dt

#NORMALIZATION
1.0e5                   lNormSI   [m]  ~ d_i so d_i ~ 1 in code units
1.0e5                   uNormSI   [m/s] tNorm = 1 s; sets cLight_SI = uNormSI

#MONITOR
50                      dnReport

#SAVELOG
1                       dnSavePic
1                       dnSavePT

#PARTICLES
2000                    nParticle per cell in X
1                       nParticle per cell in Y
1                       nParticle per cell in Z

#SAVEPLOT
1                       nPlot
z=0 var ascii pic       plotString
50                      dn
0.0                     dt
1                       dx
Pe rhoS0                plotVar

#STOP
-1                      MaxIter
5.0                     TimeMax   ~0.3 of the n=1 ion-acoustic period

#RUN
